  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}\u2061(4x+4y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}\u2061(4(x+y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={tan}^{2}\u2061(4z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}\u2061(4z)+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)={tan}^{2}\u2061(4z)+1.$",
